**Definition 8.1 (retained complete groups and exact missing relation).**
The complete directed endpoint inventory, weak-sign partition
$\mathscr G$, full matrix $A$, contrast row $\kappa$ and polytope
$\mathcal P$ remain those of FC Definitions 3.1–5.1. Every paired
group retains both independent endpoint coordinates. Reverse
singletons remain singletons, including arbitrary additional reverse
contacts. In the rank-one range $30000\le t<2000000000$, every
candidate law still must carry the mandatory negative pure coordinate
in $G_*$ as well as the mandatory reverse saturated mass from FC28.
Occupying $G_*$ solely at its saturated coordinate is insufficient.

The remaining seven-low problem asks, on each relevant complete
face and for every $r\in[r_-,r_+]$, for a vector

$$
w\ge0,\qquad Aw=B(t),\qquad \kappa^Tw=r,\qquad
\#\left\{G\in\mathscr G:\sum_{i\in G}w_i>0\right\}\le7.
\tag{RB.43}
$$

By FC Theorem 5.2 this is exactly $U_7=[r_-,r_+]$.
Neither the affine factor (RB.7), the Toeplitz matrix (RB.29),
nor the curvature estimate implies a row dependence of the full
augmented $(\Gamma,k)$ table or constructs (RB.43). Theorem 7.1
excludes a class of complete jointly attained faces at large finite
budget; it supplies no short-support law in the remaining class.
Indefinite faces, including the high-budget faces of Corollary 7.2,
retain the original compression question.

The mature moment interfaces illustrate the missing correspondence.
Di Dio–Schmüdgen, *The Moment Cone*,
[Theorems 19, 30 and 52](https://arxiv.org/html/1809.00584v1),
works with the specified finite measurable function space:
Richter's representation bound uses its dimension; Theorem 30
describes atom sets by the appropriate moment-face zero sets; the
$m-1$ refinement in Theorem 52 requires at most $m-1$
path-connected components of the normalized nonzero evaluation
image. The scalar gap factor in (RB.7) supplies neither those
full-table dimension reductions nor the required connectedness.

Nailwal–Zalar, *Gaussian Quadratures with prescribed nodes via
moment theory*,
[Theorem 3.1 and Example 3.6](https://arxiv.org/html/2412.20849v1),
requires full real univariate data through
$D=d_1+2d_2-1$, a positive definite initial moment matrix,
distinct prescribed nodes, an invertible prescribed-node localizer
and positive definiteness of its recursively generated extension.
Its factorial example with nodes $1/3,11$ fails that extension
positivity despite the invertible localizer. The mandatory $x_*$
here supplies none of the missing univariate or original
$\Gamma,K,J_t$ correspondence.

Riener–Turatti, *Quadrature rules with few nodes supported on
algebraic curves*,
[Theorem 1.1](https://arxiv.org/html/2509.06643v1),
matches full polynomial strength under its smooth plane-curve and
places-at-infinity hypotheses, or its rational parametrization degree
and real-pole hypotheses. Factoring one signed dual gap does not
map the full two-sheet rational-capacity, absolute-height tests and
directed actual labels to that interface. These primary moment and
quadrature results are cited at this applicability boundary, not
used as replacement theorems for (RB.43). The scalar
Fejér–Riesz application in Proposition 5.1 is the only external
factorization used in the exclusion. The source-specific composition
of §§2–7 is a repo-derived conditional deduction.

